WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.300
Every scientist learns that
correlation does not imply causation.

2
00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:10.192
Yet a pattern of dependencies and
independencies can rule out many causal stories,

3
00:00:10.192 --> 00:00:13.675
and sometimes it leaves
only one direction standing.

4
00:00:14.067 --> 00:00:17.260
Deciding which causal claims
are forced by the data,

5
00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:21.067
and which are merely possible,
is a precise deductive problem.

6
00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:25.725
Our paper asks how well language
models solve it, and shows that one

7
00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:29.875
change helps substantially:
making the model write down its graph.

8
00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:35.029
This is our paper,
Externalized CPDAG Summaries

9
00:00:35.029 --> 00:00:39.667
Improve LLM Causal Deduction,
accepted at NeurIPS 2026.

10
00:00:41.867 --> 00:00:45.367
Let's begin with three
variables: A, B, and C.

11
00:00:45.767 --> 00:00:48.052
Suppose all three are correlated,

12
00:00:48.052 --> 00:00:51.792
but A and C become independent
once we condition on B.

13
00:00:52.183 --> 00:00:55.246
Three causal graphs
explain this equally well:

14
00:00:55.246 --> 00:00:59.108
a chain from A to C,
the reverse chain, or B causing both.

15
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:02.874
All three imply exactly
the same independencies,

16
00:01:02.874 --> 00:01:06.600
so independence data alone
can never tell them apart.

17
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.855
Together they form a
Markov equivalence class.

18
00:01:10.135 --> 00:01:15.560
The claim that A causes B holds in only one,
so it is possible, not deducible.

19
00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:18.025
Now reverse the evidence.

20
00:01:18.305 --> 00:01:23.180
A and C are independent, but become
dependent once we condition on B.

21
00:01:23.567 --> 00:01:26.703
Only one graph fits, the lone survivor from

22
00:01:26.703 --> 00:01:29.767
our opening: A and C are both causes of B.

23
00:01:30.047 --> 00:01:35.197
Think of rain and a sprinkler, unrelated
until you learn that the lawn is wet.

24
00:01:35.477 --> 00:01:37.719
This is a collider, or v-structure,

25
00:01:37.719 --> 00:01:41.052
and its direction is forced
by the statistics alone.

26
00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:46.518
With more variables, the whole equivalence
class is summarized by a CPDAG:

27
00:01:46.518 --> 00:01:49.600
a completed partially
directed acyclic graph.

28
00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:54.105
A directed edge points the same
way in every compatible graph.

29
00:01:54.385 --> 00:01:57.710
An undirected edge marks genuine ambiguity.

30
00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:02.025
The classic PC algorithm
constructs it in three steps.

31
00:02:02.305 --> 00:02:05.855
Remove edges using
conditional independencies.

32
00:02:06.135 --> 00:02:07.935
Orient the colliders.

33
00:02:08.215 --> 00:02:12.951
Then propagate forced directions with
Meek's rules: here, C must point to D,

34
00:02:12.951 --> 00:02:16.815
because the reverse would create
a collider the data rule out.

35
00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:21.587
The decisive rule is a 'for all':
a causal hypothesis is deducible

36
00:02:21.587 --> 00:02:25.375
only if it holds in every
graph of the equivalence class.

37
00:02:25.655 --> 00:02:29.855
Here, A causes C in both graphs,
so that claim is deducible.

38
00:02:30.135 --> 00:02:33.060
E causes A in only one, so it is not.

39
00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.723
The Corr2Cause benchmark,
by Jin and colleagues,

40
00:02:37.723 --> 00:02:40.475
turns this into a test for language models.

41
00:02:40.867 --> 00:02:45.091
Each instance lists correlations
and conditional independencies

42
00:02:45.091 --> 00:02:49.717
among a few variables, then asks
whether a causal hypothesis follows.

43
00:02:49.997 --> 00:02:54.407
Of 1,162 test instances, only 180 are 'yes',

44
00:02:54.407 --> 00:03:00.822
so always answering 'no' would
already score about 85% accuracy.

45
00:03:01.102 --> 00:03:03.822
We therefore report F1 on the 'yes' class,

46
00:03:03.822 --> 00:03:07.902
which rewards finding true claims
without asserting false ones.

47
00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:13.150
Notice what the label depends on:
a hidden graph that the model never sees.

48
00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:16.680
We frame the task as
latent-object reasoning.

49
00:03:17.067 --> 00:03:21.004
In free-form chain-of-thought,
the step-by-step text a model

50
00:03:21.004 --> 00:03:24.942
writes before answering,
that object exists only implicitly.

51
00:03:25.222 --> 00:03:30.259
The global question, does the claim hold
in all compatible graphs, can quietly

52
00:03:30.259 --> 00:03:35.297
shrink to a local one: I found one graph
where it holds, so the answer is yes.

53
00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:41.233
Our method, Structured Thinking,
makes the latent object explicit.

54
00:03:41.633 --> 00:03:46.533
In the first turn, the model receives
instructions for the PC algorithm.

55
00:03:46.813 --> 00:03:50.820
It may reason freely, but must
then fill in a typed graph record:

56
00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:53.963
which pairs are connected,
what separates the rest,

57
00:03:53.963 --> 00:03:57.538
the colliders, and which edges
are directed or undirected.

58
00:03:57.933 --> 00:04:01.108
This form is enforced, not merely requested.

59
00:04:01.388 --> 00:04:04.238
Open-weight Qwen models decode under a

60
00:04:04.238 --> 00:04:07.688
regular expression derived
from a JSON schema.

61
00:04:07.968 --> 00:04:12.393
API models are held to the same
schema through strict tool calling.

62
00:04:12.783 --> 00:04:17.952
In the second turn, the model gets its graph back,
along with the original problem,

63
00:04:17.952 --> 00:04:22.499
and answers by querying it: is the
edge present, is its direction forced,

64
00:04:22.499 --> 00:04:26.858
and does the claim survive every way
of orienting the ambiguous edges?

65
00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:30.741
There is no fine-tuning and
no symbolic causal solver:

66
00:04:30.741 --> 00:04:32.875
the model does all the reasoning.

67
00:04:33.155 --> 00:04:37.655
What changes is how its intermediate
state is written down, and read back.

68
00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:43.725
A fair evaluation needs strong baselines,
not plain chain-of-thought.

69
00:04:44.117 --> 00:04:48.666
Our main baseline gives the model a
comparable PC-algorithm walkthrough,

70
00:04:48.666 --> 00:04:52.835
including colliders, Meek's rules,
and the all-graphs requirement,

71
00:04:52.835 --> 00:04:55.742
but asks for a direct
answer in a single turn.

72
00:04:56.133 --> 00:04:59.272
A second control keeps the
scaffold and two turns,

73
00:04:59.272 --> 00:05:01.783
but lets the intermediate be free prose.

74
00:05:02.063 --> 00:05:07.088
It asks whether an extra turn, rather
than the typed graph, explains the gain.

75
00:05:08.283 --> 00:05:13.333
Here is the primary comparison,
on Qwen3.5-27B.

76
00:05:13.733 --> 00:05:17.033
The strong baseline reaches 73.0 F1.

77
00:05:17.313 --> 00:05:20.363
Structured Thinking reaches 86.4.

78
00:05:20.643 --> 00:05:25.793
In our primary run, that 13.4-point
gain is statistically clear.

79
00:05:26.073 --> 00:05:30.593
But sampling matters:
across three independent runs,

80
00:05:30.593 --> 00:05:35.373
the gain ranges from 2.7 to
13.4 points, averaging 8.1.

81
00:05:35.767 --> 00:05:40.092
The two-turn prose
control reaches only 67.6.

82
00:05:40.372 --> 00:05:44.747
An extra turn of prose, even with
the same scaffold, is not enough.

83
00:05:45.133 --> 00:05:50.051
In the primary run, this is not
just a shift toward saying yes:

84
00:05:50.051 --> 00:05:55.358
missed positives fall from 61 to 28,
and false alarms from 27 to 20.

85
00:05:55.638 --> 00:05:58.963
In the other runs, the gain
comes mainly from recall.

86
00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:04.304
The gain also holds on Qwen3.6-27B,
on a reworded split with the same

87
00:06:04.304 --> 00:06:09.330
underlying graphs, and on a smaller
9B-parameter model, and it remains

88
00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:14.500
positive for Gemma and GPT-5.4-mini,
whose baselines are already strong.

89
00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:17.670
The gain is larger on bigger graphs:

90
00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:21.825
about 5 points with five variables,
and 18.6 with six.

91
00:06:22.105 --> 00:06:25.366
In single-run pilot tests on larger graphs,

92
00:06:25.366 --> 00:06:31.055
the baseline falls below 30 F1,
while Structured Thinking reaches 60 to 92.

93
00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:36.150
But is the graph actually used,
or is it just decoration?

94
00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:40.066
Scrambling the emitted
graph before the second turn

95
00:06:40.066 --> 00:06:43.650
costs 12 points of F1,
back to roughly the baseline.

96
00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:49.060
Scrambling only the skeleton, only the
colliders, or only the directed edges

97
00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:54.055
costs about 6 each, while removing the
original premise costs less than 4.

98
00:06:54.335 --> 00:06:56.585
The answer depends on the graph.

99
00:06:56.983 --> 00:07:01.483
We also audited every emitted
graph against the reference CPDAG.

100
00:07:01.763 --> 00:07:07.263
Skeleton F1 is 96, and about three
quarters of graphs match exactly.

101
00:07:07.543 --> 00:07:12.866
And accuracy tracks graph quality:
97% when the graph is exact,

102
00:07:12.866 --> 00:07:16.668
91% when it contains a
consistency violation.

103
00:07:17.067 --> 00:07:19.342
Ablations tell the same story.

104
00:07:19.622 --> 00:07:23.747
Without the PC-algorithm instructions,
performance collapses.

105
00:07:24.027 --> 00:07:27.438
Removing constrained
decoding costs 9.5 points,

106
00:07:27.438 --> 00:07:32.302
and removing the free reasoning
phase costs 14, at this model size.

107
00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:36.350
Our claims are deliberately bounded.

108
00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:42.130
The independencies are given as clean
statements, not estimated from noisy data.

109
00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:46.789
The schema guarantees a parseable
graph, not a certified one:

110
00:07:46.789 --> 00:07:50.235
about 1 output in 5 has
a consistency violation.

111
00:07:50.515 --> 00:07:54.382
The method bundles components
we have not fully separated,

112
00:07:54.382 --> 00:07:57.715
and several secondary
results rest on single runs.

113
00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:02.375
The broader lesson is a design
principle worth testing elsewhere.

114
00:08:02.655 --> 00:08:06.155
Identify the latent object
that defines the label.

115
00:08:06.435 --> 00:08:08.060
Externalize it.

116
00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:10.140
Constrain its form.

117
00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:14.045
Then test whether the final
answer truly depends on it.

118
00:08:15.833 --> 00:08:18.558
Code, prompts, and schemas are public.

119
00:08:18.838 --> 00:08:21.358
Correlation does not imply causation,

120
00:08:21.358 --> 00:08:24.763
but sometimes a pattern
of correlations forces it.

121
00:08:25.043 --> 00:08:26.818
Thank you for watching.
